<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choe Chung-heon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1149">1149</a>–<a href="/years/1219">1219</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choe Chung-heon was a Korean general who seized power in Goryeo in 1196 and founded a military house that ruled the kingdom for four generations. He deposed two kings, ruled through his own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyojeong-dogam">Directorate General for Policy Formulation</a> and private guards, and left the kings as figureheads. His descendants governed until the last Choe dictator was killed in 1258.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-chung-heon.jpg" alt="Choe Chung-heon" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dick Thomas Johnson from Tokyo, Japan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tokyo_National_Museum_Toyokan_(52504076964).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ch'oe Ch'unghŏn was the son of Supreme General Ch'oe Wŏnho and Lady Yu, and is thought to have been born in Gaeseong or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>. His family descended from the Confucian scholar Ch'oe Ch'iwŏn, but became the Ubong Ch'oe clan when his father was given Ubong as an ancestral seat. Like his father he entered the military. He first held a minor post in the Agency for Royal Liquors, came to notice fighting Cho Wich'ong's rebellion in 1174, and later served as royal inspector of Gyeongsang Jinju Province until an impeachment stalled his career for several years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His chance came in April 1196, when Yi Chiyŏng, a son of the military ruler Yi Ŭimin, took a pet pigeon from Ch'oe's younger brother Ch'oe Ch'ungsu. The brothers, with their nephew Pak Chinjae and others, beheaded Yi Ŭimin while he was away at Mount Mita, won over General Paek Chonyu in the capital, and killed or exiled Yi's followers among the officials. To justify the coup they presented the king with a ten-point reform programme, the Bongsasipjo, which called for fair taxes, fewer officials, frugality and curbs on monks and temples.</p><p>In 1197 Ch'oe confined King Myeongjong to Changrak Palace and set Sinjong on the throne. When Sinjong fell ill in 1204 Ch'oe chose his son Huijong, deposed him after a plot against his own life in 1211, and installed Gangjong and then Gojong. He had his brother Ch'oe Ch'ungsu beheaded in a struggle over a royal marriage, crushed a revolt by Pak Chinjae in 1207, a Silla restoration movement around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> and a rising of slaves led by Manjeok, and based his power on a private guard, the Tobang, and on the Directorate-General for Policy Formulation, set up in 1209 to hunt down conspirators.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ch'oe named his elder son, Ch'oe U, as successor after U defeated his brother Ch'oe Hyang in a sword fight, and retired. He died on 29 October 1219, a year after a stroke, and his funeral was recorded as like a king's. The system he built passed to Ch'oe U, Ch'oe Hang and Ch'oe Ŭi. Unlike earlier military rulers he drew Confucian scholars such as Yi Kyubo into government, though his regime is judged essentially a continuation of the military regimes before it. Its private army, the Sambyeolcho, rebelled after the regime fell and was defeated by a Mongol-Goryeo army.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ch'oe Ch'unghŏn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a coup in 1170 military strongmen ruled; from 1196 <b>Choe Chung-heon</b> and his descendants governed through their own Directorate General for Policy Formulation while kings reigned in name only, until 1258.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1196 <b>Choe Chung-heon</b> seized power and founded a military house that ruled until 1258, and royal authority was restored only in 1270.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,126.55&amp;year=1194" aria-label="Kaesong on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7704 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Choe Chung-heon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8154 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8154 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,126.55&amp;year=1194">What was here in 1194? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
